<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 825 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">Obelisk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> is a black limestone monument from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, erected around 825 BCE and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Its relief panels show tribute brought to the Assyrian king from five lands; one panel shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a> of Israel, or his envoy, prostrate before Shalmaneser, the only known contemporary image of a king of Israel or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-obelisk-of-shalmaneser-iii.jpg" alt="Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III,_9th_century_BC,_from_Nimrud,_Iraq._The_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Black Obelisk is a black limestone Neo-Assyrian sculpture with many scenes in bas-relief and inscriptions. It comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, ancient Kalhu, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and commemorates the deeds of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a>, who reigned from 858 to 824 BCE. It is one of two complete Neo-Assyrian obelisks yet discovered, the other being the much earlier White Obelisk of Ashurnasirpal I.</p><p>It was erected as a public monument in 825 BCE, at a time of civil war, in the central square of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>. Some features of the work suggest that it may have been commissioned by the king's commander-in-chief, Dayyan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> carries twenty relief scenes, five on each side. They show five subdued kings bringing tribute and prostrating themselves before the Assyrian king. From top to bottom they are Sua of Gilzanu, in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; Yaua of Bit Omri, that is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omride-dynasty">House of Omri</a>; an unnamed ruler of Musri, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>; Marduk-apil-usur of Suhi, on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>; and Qalparunda of Patin, in the Antakya region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Each scene occupies four panels around the monument, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> caption above.</p><p>Long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscriptions at the top and bottom record the annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a>. They list the military campaigns that the king and his commander-in-chief led each year until the thirty-first year of the reign. The reference to Parsua is the first known reference to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The archaeologist Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austen-henry-layard">Austen Henry Layard</a> discovered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> in 1846. It is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, and several other museums hold cast replicas. The second register from the top is thought to hold the earliest surviving picture of a biblical figure. Its caption reads that the king received the tribute of Iaua, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, gold, a golden bowl, a golden vase with pointed bottom, golden tumblers, golden buckets, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, a staff for a king and spears. The tribute is dated to about 841 BCE.</p><p>In 1850 Henry Rawlinson read the name as Yahua, son of Hubiri, a prince he could not place. On 21 August 1851 the Reverend Edward Hincks identified him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>, king of Israel, and this identification is now the commonly held position among biblical archaeologists. Scholars including George Smith, P. Kyle McCarter and Edwin R. Thiele have questioned it, citing the fact that Jehu was not an Omride, along with problems of transliteration and chronology.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mesha Stele names ‘Omri, king of Israel’, the Kurkh Monolith names Ahab, and the <b>Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III</b> shows Jehu, or his envoy, prostrate before the Assyrian king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
